#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046949a3-90df-516c-995b-b46249819ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551673} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8c1fd7-4dd8-502c-b528-815a2db63d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551810} {:answer/id #uuid "4a051744-2fdf-5555-a542-727518fba033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551844} {:answer/id #uuid "4dde6904-218e-5e1b-bfed-644cb9dc6af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551851} {:answer/id #uuid "f19495bb-f541-5c6f-9b64-66ca79e589a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "046949a3-90df-516c-995b-b46249819ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551673} :answer-field/id #uuid "442a3cda-75e4-511b-a475-bfba07bcff50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84086ce8-3230-56a5-93a0-7e75dffe8598" :question/math-academy-id "q-331370" :question/problem "![](images/87/8715fffa8fec9468edef2194b75842cb79de3bc9a7db6c0d8d23f44c6eb5368b.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40549b82-d47f-561c-8931-d7d35ef461cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444124} {:answer/id #uuid "62f8b741-a9bc-5f56-8242-c18d258a8e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444130} {:answer/id #uuid "85b1285c-0fbc-5e41-b4e0-700ef6b3681e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444132} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444133} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7d8b7f-1e93-5e81-b22e-1d876ddc07f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444133} :answer-field/id #uuid "540f62cb-7d1e-5455-b752-8d1fe82b863d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e106ec5-d244-5095-9a3e-86feed9bed83" :question/math-academy-id "q-332687" :question/problem "![](images/39/390321da0e4c3367d121a1561ccc41ca4960c912ff53d909acd1c90f505758ce.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c87346-c0ee-53c3-94ac-95b49a1375c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302693} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1993f9-8087-5c6b-a1ad-dedd2a035b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303328} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e742a2-b236-57f3-8979-a3b9a473fc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303672} {:answer/id #uuid "d10f1558-a4b7-543d-abeb-1abd68004cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303745} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1b612c-1d62-5b79-aa3b-7cf7a714c584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5e742a2-b236-57f3-8979-a3b9a473fc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303672} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe78fafd-1fb0-5c2f-9ba1-e3c0b222dbf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd958f9f-ce7e-5925-b2cc-b90dad87e64d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332710" :question/problem "![](images/83/83a565accc834d7c16091de22eb43ba80c0ce9c4290f567d1e0ccc009beca565.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419971} {:answer/id #uuid "7adc6eff-a523-5b7e-8d97-de46f768fe8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30d392d02e60c7bb8d76f7d4773a1d016d7cfa6343c893fb644428fde58571bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419975} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc531d0-6c2d-5648-b378-65082454a6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/0781cf2ca92efb10c050102b8dd034e004bb8da29b743fb154e2cc6aaf374c28.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419990} {:answer/id #uuid "d44e5877-6f35-50c1-a9af-6f5adefc791b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90addbe477ec66925586a5f8da3921dcaa6e22ed9ea4c1fb65f8b91095ad4850.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419992} {:answer/id #uuid "e83d540c-00e0-5931-b515-134723ba6e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/139efbf21eb0cf93de2b041a63da84ac802898a810031c89471c620f0cb98ed6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419971} :answer-field/id #uuid "0521532e-d96e-5d0f-ae24-3ae545170424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92828ad5-f63d-58d9-843f-369518e02ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333188" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/36/362ee182f39f9d5cc1e7597f7e836a6ce802b4762a03c347c5701c0830f3d523.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-5t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-5{c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{5{c}_{1}}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461def2d-f65b-5702-81dc-9c688ad9792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391171} {:answer/id #uuid "64590024-0b5d-5509-b3ed-cec5555ef505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391172} {:answer/id #uuid "e9097811-7f04-5636-ac94-08d654db1f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "461def2d-f65b-5702-81dc-9c688ad9792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391171} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78979b2-0930-5c53-983d-999f23a46873" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5edfc04a-0185-57d1-a0a8-90f14d6bb9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391175} {:answer/id #uuid "83c65d7a-dd3e-5392-bb3e-959427004b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5edfc04a-0185-57d1-a0a8-90f14d6bb9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391175} :answer-field/id #uuid "110033c6-9468-57ae-9c4b-2409f3360843" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ad3d999-893c-5b32-86ed-3c76b9777309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391178} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf150cb-9ddf-51fd-a897-b57d640d5664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad3d999-893c-5b32-86ed-3c76b9777309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391178} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb6a2c1-2961-5d3d-8a0c-af139a57aa82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7685d561-af7c-5963-8ff0-5f1c98ad02e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333227" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b4/b406c864e820819c5ae6f548c7a025a1e1d8197fb785ea5689080034e04210cb.png)"}]}
